<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">First Balkan War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1912">1912</a>–<a href="/years/1913">1913</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The First Balkan War was fought from October 1912 to May 1913 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. The allies took almost all Ottoman lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, and Albania declared independence. Quarrels over the spoils led directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war.jpg" alt="First Balkan War" width="320" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Avidius · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balkanskata_voina_Photobox.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> had secured their independence by 1867, confirmed by the Treaty of Berlin in 1878. Between 1878 and 1912 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and Serbia sponsored insurgent groups in Ottoman Macedonia that fought the government and each other in the Macedonian Struggle. The Young Turk Revolution of July 1908 revived the question of whether Ottoman rule could last. By 1912 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, known as the sick man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, was still fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodecanese">Dodecanese</a>, and peace came only on 15 October 1912, days after the Balkan war began. Bulgaria was the strongest of the four states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a>, with some 600,000 men mobilised, while Greece, thought the weakest, was the only member with a meaningful navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> declared war on 8 October 1912. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a> the Ottoman command had wrongly expected the main Bulgarian army to go to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> defeated the Ottoman East Army on the Edirne-Kırklareli line. In Macedonia the Greek Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Bulgarians won great victories at Kirkkilise, Lüleburgaz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumanovo">Kumanovo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bitola">Monastir</a>. The Greek fleet took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lemnos">Lemnos</a> and kept the Ottoman fleet in the Dardanelles.</p><p>Adrianople fell on 11 March 1913 after a two-week bombardment and a final assault by the Bulgarian Second Army and two Serbian divisions. Ottoman casualties in the whole Adrianople campaign reached 23,000 dead, and the Ottoman command lost all hope of regaining the initiative. On 23 April 1913 the garrison of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shkoder">Shkodër</a> surrendered, starved, to the Montenegrins. The Ottoman government had declared war with its mobilisation only partly complete, and 580,000 Ottoman soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> faced 912,000 of the League.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-london-1915">Treaty of London</a> ended the war on 30 May 1913. All Ottoman territory west of the Enez-Kıyıköy line passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-league">Balkan League</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a> was declared independent. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> lost 83 per cent of their European territories and 69 per cent of their European population. Many civilians were killed or driven from their homes, and both sides committed war crimes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, which occupied most of the territory meant for Albania, withdrew only reluctantly. Greece and Serbia signed a military alliance against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> on 1 May 1913, and Bulgaria, disputing the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonia-region">Macedonia</a>, attacked its former allies on 16 June 1913, beginning the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-balkan-war">Second Balkan War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “First Balkan War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the <b>First Balkan War</b> it took Pristina and Novi Pazar, defeated the Ottoman army at the Battle of Kumanovo and conquered Skopje and the Kosovo vilayet.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Farewell of Slavianka&quot; is a Russian patriotic march written by the composer Vasily Agapkin in honour of Slavic women seeing their husbands off to the <b>First Balkan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/farewell-of-slavianka">Farewell of Slavianka</a></li><li><blockquote>In Vlorë, Ismail Qemali raised the Albanian flag and proclaimed independence from the Ottoman Empire during the <b>First Balkan War</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-28">28 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>When the <b>First Balkan War</b> began, the Greek navy arrived in November 1912 and the island joined the Kingdom of Greece.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-icaria">Free State of Icaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Bulgaria joined the Balkan League and won the <b>First Balkan War</b> in 1912, but its attack on Serbia and Greece in 1913 led to defeat in the Second Balkan War and loss of most of its gains.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bulgaria">Kingdom of Bulgaria</a></li><li><blockquote>In the <b>First Balkan War</b> Montenegro besieged and took Scutari in 1913, but the powers forced it to hand the town to the new Albania; it did gain much of the Sandžak and Metohija, including Peć and Đakovica.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-montenegro">Kingdom of Montenegro</a></li></ul></section>
